# Gitleaks allowlist.
#
# Every entry here is a finding a human looked at and decided was not a
# credential. Entries are pinned to a specific commit:file:rule:line, so an
# allowlist can never quietly cover a *different* leak in the same file.
#
# Adding an entry requires a reason. An allowlist without reasons becomes a
# place where real findings go to be forgotten.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# e1ac3c0 — AWS's published documentation key in a test fixture.
#
# The value is the example access key ID from AWS's own public documentation
# (the AKIA…EXAMPLE one). It authenticates nothing and never has. It was used
# in a test proving that Mykronos scrubs an unredacted secret out of an
# archived Gitleaks record.
#
# The literal value is deliberately not written here. The first version of
# this file quoted it in this very comment, and Gitleaks promptly flagged the
# allowlist — which is the correct behaviour, and a good argument for
# describing an allowlisted value rather than reproducing it.
#
# Our own Gitleaks job flagged it on the first run against this repository,
# which is the scanner working correctly: it is credential-shaped, and being
# credential-shaped in a repo is the thing the rule is for.
#
# Fixed forward — the fixture now uses a sentinel that is not credential-
# shaped — but the original line remains in commit e1ac3c0. History is not
# being rewritten for it: this repository is public and already cloned, and
# rewriting shared history to hide a value AWS publishes in its own
# documentation is a worse trade than one audited allowlist entry.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
